00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.710 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  friday september 2nd  i'm zoe thomas for the wall street  journal  prosecutors are calling it their first  case targeting insider trading in  non-fungible tokens  in june nathaniel chastain a former  employee of the nft marketplace open c  was accused of benefiting from knowledge  about whether the prices of some digital  art would go up  it's a novel case and one that could  test the bounds of fraud law in the u.s  but some lawyers say it could set a  dangerous precedent that goes beyond  digital assets joining us to discuss  this is richard vander ford from the  wsj's risk and compliance journal hi  richard thanks for being here thanks for  having me  so start with reminding us what happened  with nathaniel chastain  so nathaniel chastain was an employee of  openc which is a very large nft  marketplace he was a product manager  and one of his duties was to pick what  nfts for sale would be put on the front  of the website featured on the website  and so he obviously knew what was going  to go on the website and he's accused of  using that knowledge to buy up these  nfts before they went on the website  they would go on the website it would  drive interest and he would quickly sell  them to make big profits according to  prosecutors  and what has his legal team said about  these charges he's pleaded not guilty  his legal team isn't talking on the  record to reporters but they've asked  that the charges be dismissed  he's been charged under this wire fraud  statute and not under securities fraud  why is that important in this case  when most people think about insider  trading they think about insider trading  in stocks which is often charged under a  securities fraud statute wire fraud is a  really old law it's very broad it  encompasses all kinds of what people  would think of as crooked behavior and  prosecutors really like it because it's  very adaptable to new kinds of scams  so this case is really a test of whether  his conduct can be charged under this  wire fraud law that is very broad  what have opponents of this kind of case  said how do they feel about that kind of  approach  a group of prominent new york defense  attorneys have said that prosecutors are  going too far in using the wire fraud  statute this way and potentially  criminalizing a lot of normal workplace  conduct or minor misconduct that maybe  shouldn't be a crime  under the legal theory being used by  prosecutors here the crime is really in  taking the information from his employer  and using it to make money  so these defense lawyers have come up  with a bunch of scenarios that most  people wouldn't think of as criminal  like if companies headquarters were  moving  and an executive used that information  to buy a house before the market heated  up or if someone wrote a tell-all book  about their boss in defiance of a  non-disclosure agreement  these defense lawyers are saying under  the prosecutor's theory in this case  that kind of taking of confidential  workplace information could be a crime  but it probably shouldn't be  they've asked the judge to dismiss this  case do we know when we're going to kind  of get a reaction from the judge what we  expect to hear from him yeah so there'll  be a whole bunch of hearings on whether  to dismiss the case that kind of remedy  dismissing a case before goes to a jury  is really rare judges are really  inclined to give prosecutors the benefit  of the doubt and let their cases go  before a jury  that said there's appeals courts in new  york as well and the appeals court could  be more skeptical but i think it could  take months or years to fully unfold  what does the prosecution said about why  they're taking this tactic kind of going  wire fraud as opposed to securities  fraud they haven't outlined their their  legal theory or their sort of tactical  legal decision but  if you go under a wire fraud theory you  arguably don't have to prove that a  security was involved and there's a lot  of uncertainty about whether digital  assets like nfts or cryptocurrency can  be considered a security a lot of  differences of legal opinion about that  so under this theory the crime is just  that he allegedly took  information from his workplace and used  that to profit and you don't have to get  into a question of well is this a  security or is this not a security  i wonder though you know this group of  defense attorneys is saying it could set  a precedent for workplace misconduct  could it set a precedent for kind of how  we think about digital assets and how  crimes around digital assets are  prosecuted yeah a win for the  prosecutors here would be huge it's the  manhattan federal prosecutors who  brought this case and they typically are  very aggressive and obviously there's a  lot of kind of digital asset work being  centered in manhattan and in new york so  if the prosecutors win  and an appeals court agrees with them  especially then  this theory would be vindicated and  assets that people might not have  thought of as being an insider tradable  asset will de facto be something you  could insider trade and violate the law  in that way  you know nfts were so popular last year  they were surging in value now that's  not the case anymore will that in any  way impact this trial  i don't think prosecutors are going to  walk away from a case like this because  like nfts turn out to be sort of an  irrelevant thing i think once they start  on a case they want to win  and i think the prosecutors in manhattan  they want to be seen policing these  digital assets they made a show of  saying this is the first ever nft  insider trading case they brought a case  the month after this in cryptocurrency  insider trading and the relative  popularity of nfts isn't going to really  change the way the trial goes  all right that's our reporter richard  vander ford thanks so much for joining  us richard thank you  and that's it for tech news briefing  this week our producer is julie chang  our supervising producer is chris  sinsley our executive producer is kateri  yocum and i'm your host zoe thomas  before we go a quick programming note  we'll be off this coming monday for  labor day but we'll be back on tuesday  thanks for listening and have a great  weekend  [Music]  you

